Thanks — I can help you get ready. A couple quick clarifying questions before I go into specifics:
- When you say “my last blood pressure was 72%,” did you mean something else (for example a single BP number like 172/72, or an ejection fraction of 72%)? If you can share your most recent BP readings and last A1c (if you have them) I can tailor the questions more precisely.

Below I’ve organized: (A) focused questions to bring to your PCP about hypertension management, (B) guidance on when to consider referral to endocrinology (and suggested questions to ask about a referral to Phoenix Endocrinology), (C) tests and items to bring, and (D) a few suggested phrases/scripts you can use at the visit.

A — Hypertension management: key topics and questions to ask Dr. Amy Foster
- BP target and rationale
  - “What blood pressure target should I aim for given my type 2 diabetes, heart failure history, and age?”
  - Note to ask: many guidelines recommend a goal near <130/80 for people with diabetes if tolerated, but targets can be individualized with heart failure, orthostatic symptoms, or frailty.
- Current control and assessment
  - “Is my blood pressure currently controlled on lisinopril 20 mg? Do you want to see a home BP log or order ambulatory monitoring?”
  - “How many home readings should I take and when (morning/evening, sitting/standing)?”
- Medication strategy and interactions
  - “Given my meds (lisinopril, metoprolol, apixaban), are any changes needed for better BP control or safety?”
  - “Should we consider adding or changing an agent (thiazide-like diuretic, calcium channel blocker, or others) — and which would be safest with my heart failure and kidney function?”
- Safety checks
  - “Should I be checked for orthostatic hypotension? What symptoms should prompt immediate contact (dizziness, lightheadedness, falls)?”
  - “How often should we check kidney function and electrolytes while titrating BP meds?”
- Interaction with heart-failure care
  - “Do you want cardiology involved for blood-pressure decisions because of my history of heart failure? Should I have an updated echocardiogram or BNP?”
- Monitoring and follow-up
  - “How often should I monitor BP at home and when should I return for follow-up after any medication change?”

B — Endocrinology referral: when to consider it, and what to ask about Phoenix Endocrinology
Reasons to consider an endocrinology referral:
- Persistent elevated A1c despite optimization of lifestyle and primary-care medication adjustments
- Need to start or adjust complex medications (insulin initiation, complex basal-bolus regimens)
- Frequent hypoglycemia or large glucose variability
- Rapidly rising A1c or new/ progressive complications (significant albuminuria, retinopathy)
- Need for advanced therapy (GLP-1 receptor agonists, SGLT2 inhibitors) evaluation or for specialized counseling about cardiometabolic medications that affect heart failure/renal outcomes

Questions to ask Dr. Foster about a referral:
- “Given my age (52), diabetes, and heart-failure history, would you recommend a referral to endocrinology (Phoenix Endocrinology) now or only if my A1c/BP aren’t at goal?”
- “What specific problems would prompt an endocrinology referral in my case?”
- “Would a diabetes educator or nutritionist be useful before or instead of a specialist visit?”
- “Are SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP-1 agonists appropriate for me given my heart-failure history? Should we discuss these with cardiology/endocrinology?”

If you want me to draft a one-line referral request you can read:
- “Dr. Foster — can you please refer me to Phoenix Endocrinology for specialized diabetes management and consideration of cardio-protective diabetes therapies? My recent A1c is X (or unknown), and I have type 2 diabetes with heart failure and am on lisinopril, metoprolol, apixaban, and atorvastatin.”

C — Tests, data, and items to bring to the visit
Bring:
- A printed/phone log of your home BP readings (dates/times, posture)
- A current medication list (doses/timing) and all pill bottles
- Recent A1c if you have it, or request one at the visit
- Any recent labs: BMP/creatinine/eGFR, potassium, lipid panel, urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio
- Weight and any recent weight changes; list of symptoms (dizziness, swelling, chest pain, shortness of breath)
- Date of last eye exam and foot exam

Ask/order:
- A1c, fasting glucose (if needed)
- Comprehensive metabolic panel (creatinine, eGFR, electrolytes)
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio
- Lipid panel
- ECG or echocardiogram if the cardiology / heart-failure status is unclear or not recent
- Consider ambulatory or home BP monitoring if readings are inconsistent

D — Suggested quick scripts to use during the appointment
- To open the discussion about BP: “I want to review my blood pressure plan. With my diabetes and heart-failure history, what target should we aim for, and is my current lisinopril dose adequate?”
- To request referral: “Do you think I should see an endocrinologist at Phoenix Endocrinology for our diabetes management, or should we try additional changes here first?”
- To coordinate meds for HF and diabetes: “Given my heart-failure history, are there diabetes medicines (SGLT2s) that could help my heart and kidneys? Would you like cardiology input before starting one?”

Red flags (contact PCP/ED sooner):
- New or worsening shortness of breath, chest pain, sudden lightheadedness or fainting, very high BP readings (e.g., systolic >180 or diastolic >120), or signs of bleeding while on apixaban.

Would you like me to:
- Draft a short message/email to Dr. Foster requesting these tests and/or a referral to Phoenix Endocrinology?
- Or help you format a BP log or list of questions to print and bring to the visit?

If you confirm what “72%” referred to and give recent A1c/BP readings (if available), I’ll tailor the recommendations and suggested phrasing.

