This is a good plan — I can help you prepare focused questions and a checklist so your annual physical with Dr. Amy Foster is efficient and covers hypertension and whether you should be referred to Phoenix Endocrinology. Before I go further: you wrote “My last blood pressure was 72%.” Can you clarify what that means — was that a single BP reading (e.g., 172/?? or 72/??), a percentage (e.g., 72% of goal), or something else? That detail will help me tailor the recommendations.

Below are helpful items to bring and things to ask, organized and prioritized.

What to bring to the visit
- Current medication list (name, dose, frequency). Include apixaban, metoprolol, lisinopril, atorvastatin, any diabetes meds (names/doses), OTCs, and supplements.
- Recent home blood pressure log (at least 1–2 weeks, morning and evening readings if possible).
- CPAP usage info (hours/night, mask fit, problems) for sleep apnea.
- Any recent labs/results (A1c, BMP/creatinine/eGFR, lipid panel, urine albumin).
- Weight and any recent weight changes; symptom list (dizziness, lightheadedness, syncope, palpitations, chest pain, dyspnea, edema, hypoglycemia episodes).
- Dates of last echo/cardiology visit if you have heart-failure follow-up.

High-priority things to discuss about hypertension (questions to ask Dr. Foster)
- “What is my target blood pressure given my age (52), diabetes, and history of heart failure?” (Ask for a numeric goal, e.g., <130/80 vs individualized target.)
- “Based on my home BP readings, do we need to change my antihypertensive regimen?” (Show the BP log.)
- “Are my current BP medicines (metoprolol 25 mg and lisinopril 20 mg) appropriate for both blood pressure and heart-failure management, or should we adjust doses or add another agent?”
- “Should we check orthostatic vitals today (lying/standing) given my meds and any dizziness?” (Important because multiple CV meds + anticoagulation raise fall/hypotension risk.)
- “Would ambulatory (24-hour) BP monitoring or more home monitoring be useful to confirm control or detect nocturnal hypertension?”
- “Does my sleep apnea treatment need to be reassessed because poorly treated OSA can worsen BP?” (Bring CPAP adherence data.)
- “Are there interactions or bleeding concerns between my BP meds and apixaban I should know about?”
- “Given my heart-failure history, is a diuretic indicated or contraindicated right now? If we add/adjust diuretics, how will we monitor kidney function and electrolytes?”

Suggested labs and tests to request (if not already recent)
- A1c (diabetes control)
- Basic metabolic panel (creatinine, electrolytes, eGFR) — especially if changing ACEi/diuretic or starting SGLT2i
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (kidney damage screening)
- Lipid panel
- TSH (often included in annual)
- If heart-failure care: BNP/NT-proBNP if symptoms changed, or a recent echocardiogram if >1–2 years old or symptoms changed
- Consider ambulatory BP monitor or home BP log review

When to consider referral to an endocrinologist (reasons to ask Dr. Foster for a referral)
- Poor glycemic control despite optimization (for example, A1c persistently above the target Dr. Foster set — many PCPs refer if A1c remains high despite multiple med classes).
- Frequent or severe hypoglycemia or wide glucose variability.
- Need to start or intensify insulin therapy or consider complex regimens.
- Complex medication decisions (e.g., initiating GLP-1 RA or SGLT2 inhibitor in the context of heart failure/CKD) if you or your PCP want specialty input.
- Evidence of progressive microvascular complications (rapidly worsening albuminuria, retinopathy, neuropathy).
- Desire for specialized diabetes care (weight-focused therapy, newer agents, or advanced counseling).

Notes specific to your conditions and meds (topics to ask about)
- SGLT2 inhibitors: these reduce heart-failure hospitalization and help kidney outcomes; ask whether one is appropriate for you given your HF and diabetes and whether renal function supports it.
- GLP-1 receptor agonists: discuss if weight loss and CV risk reduction are goals.
- Beta-blocker and ACE inhibitor are pillars of HF therapy — confirm these doses are optimized for HF (cardiology may be involved).
- Anticoagulation (apixaban): confirm reason for anticoagulation (e.g., atrial fibrillation, VTE) and whether BP control or bleeding risk requires medication adjustment or monitoring.
- Sleep apnea: poor CPAP adherence can worsen BP and HF — if you’re not using CPAP consistently, ask about re-titration or mask options.

Sample phrasing you can use at the visit
- “Given my diabetes, heart failure, and current meds, what is my specific BP target and how do we get there safely?”
- “Do you think adding an SGLT2 inhibitor would help my heart failure and diabetes? If yes, can we check labs today to see if it’s safe?”
- “I’m having [describe symptoms] — could that be from low BP from my meds? Should we check orthostatics or change doses?”
- “Would you refer me to Phoenix Endocrinology if my A1c is X, or if we need a complex medication change?”

Red flags to mention immediately
- New chest pain, worsening shortness of breath, sudden confusion, sudden weakness, fainting, or very low BPs — seek urgent care.
- Recurrent severe hypoglycemia or major bleeding while on apixaban.

Do you want me to:
- Draft a concise message/email to Dr. Foster summarizing these concerns and requesting labs/referral? (I can draft and save/send, if you want.)
- Create a one-page checklist you can print and bring to the visit?
- Help you word a specific request for a referral to Phoenix Endocrinology?

And please clarify what “72%” meant for your last blood pressure so I can tailor targets and suggestions precisely.

