This is a great plan — I can help you prepare. First, a quick clarification: you wrote “My last blood pressure was 72%.” Do you mean the last reading was 72 mmHg (systolic or diastolic?), 72/??, or do you mean something else (e.g., 72% of goal)? That will change the recommendations about BP control and safety.

Below I’ve organized suggested things to review, tests to request, specific questions to bring to Dr. Amy Foster, and guidance on when an endocrinology referral to Phoenix Endocrinology would be reasonable.

1) Quick summary of your relevant history (so you can copy/paste to your PCP if helpful)
- Age: 52
- Type 2 diabetes
- Sleep apnea (presumably on CPAP — mention if you use it)
- Heart failure (please note if HFrEF vs HFpEF and most recent EF if known)
- Current meds you listed: apixaban 5 mg BID, metoprolol 25 mg, lisinopril 20 mg, atorvastatin 40 mg
- Concern: hypertension management and whether endocrinology referral is needed

2) Clarify before the visit
- Confirm the BP number you meant (72 mmHg systolic would be very low; 72% of target is ambiguous).
- Confirm whether you use CPAP and how adherent you are (hours/night) — this affects BP control.
- Know your latest A1c and most recent labs (creatinine/eGFR, urine albumin) if available.

3) Tests and checks to request at your annual physical (or ask your PCP to order before/at the visit)
- A1c
- Fasting lipid panel
- Basic metabolic panel (creatinine, electrolytes, glucose) and eGFR — important for apixaban and ACE inhibitor dosing
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) — for diabetic kidney disease screening
- Liver tests if not done recently (statin safety)
- ECG (if not recent) and consider BNP/NT-proBNP if heart failure symptoms or management change
- Weight/BMI, orthostatic BP check (sit/stand), and home BP log review if you have it
- Foot exam and referral for annual diabetic eye exam (retinal) if not up to date
- Medication reconciliation (all OTC, supplements) and side effects

4) Hypertension-management questions to bring to Dr. Foster
- What target BP should I be aiming for, given my diabetes and history of heart failure? (e.g., many guidelines suggest <130/80 if tolerated, but it’s individualized)
- My recent BP readings have been [insert your readings/home log]. Is my current regimen (lisinopril 20 mg + metoprolol 25 mg) adequate or should we adjust/add another agent?
- Could my symptoms (dizziness, lightheadedness, fatigue) be from low BP or medication side effects? Should we check orthostatic BPs today?
- Should we use ambulatory BP monitoring or a home BP target and how many readings should I take each day?
- How does my sleep apnea treatment (CPAP) factor into blood pressure control? If I’m not using CPAP consistently, would improving adherence likely help my BP?
- Given my heart-failure history, are there heart-failure-specific BP goals or medications I should be on (for example, do I need an ARNI, MRA, or SGLT2i — if applicable to my EF)?
- Are there any interactions or safety concerns between my BP meds, apixaban, and other drugs I take?
- If we add or change antihypertensives, how should we monitor labs (creatinine, potassium) and how soon?

5) When to consider referral to endocrinology (Phoenix Endocrinology)
Referral is reasonable if any of these apply — you can ask Dr. Foster if any apply to you:
- Persistent A1c above target despite lifestyle changes and stepwise medication adjustments (e.g., A1c >7–8%, depending on your individualized target)
- Need for initiation or complex management of injectable therapies (insulin, GLP-1 receptor agonists) or frequent hypoglycemia
- Rapidly progressive or advanced microvascular disease (retinopathy, neuropathy, albuminuria) or declining kidney function (eGFR <60 or rising creatinine)
- Complex comorbid conditions that affect diabetes treatment choice — you have heart failure (some diabetes drugs have cardiovascular and heart-failure benefits, e.g., SGLT2 inhibitors) and may benefit from specialty input
- Difficult-to-control glucose with multi-drug regimens, medication intolerance, or if you want specialized counseling (intensified lifestyle/weight management)
- If you/your PCP want a second opinion about advanced diabetes therapies (GLP-1, SGLT2, insulin) and cardiovascular risk reduction

Suggested way to ask your PCP about the referral:
- “Given my age, T2D, and history of heart failure, would you consider referring me to endocrinology for medication optimization (for example, consideration of SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP‑1 therapy) or for specialized diabetes management? If not, what clinical/lab thresholds would prompt a referral?”

6) Diabetes-specific points to raise
- Ask whether SGLT2 inhibitors are appropriate for you: these agents can improve heart-failure outcomes and renal protection in many people with diabetes and heart failure — ask whether this class is appropriate given your kidney function and anticoagulation.
- Ask about GLP-1 receptor agonists for cardiovascular risk reduction and weight management if relevant.
- Confirm whether your apixaban dose is still appropriate given current kidney function (ask PCP to review renal function for anticoagulant dosing).
- Review hypoglycemia risk (especially if you’re on agents that can cause it) and how to recognize and treat it.

7) Safety and monitoring plan to request
- Agree on a home-BP monitoring schedule (e.g., twice daily for 7 days before the visit) and a threshold when you should call the clinic (very high readings, symptomatic low BP).
- Schedule how often labs should be repeated (A1c every 3–6 months while changing therapy, kidney function every 6–12 months or sooner if meds change).
- If meds are changed, ask when to recheck creatinine and potassium (commonly within 1–2 weeks after starting or changing ACEi/ARB).

8) Coordination with cardiology / other specialties
- Because of your heart-failure history, ask whether cardiology should be looped in for changes that affect heart-failure therapy (e.g., SGLT2 initiation, ARNI, or changes to beta-blocker/ACE inhibitor).
- Confirm scheduling for annual ophthalmology and podiatry/foot checks.

9) Practical items to bring or prepare for your appointment
- A list of current medications (dose and frequency)
- Home BP log (last 2–4 weeks) and the device brand if possible
- Recent lab results if you have them
- Any symptoms you’ve been experiencing (dizziness, lightheadedness, swelling, shortness of breath, hypoglycemia episodes)
- A copy of your CPAP usage (hours/night) if applicable

10) Would you like help with any of these next steps?
- I can draft a short message/email to Dr. Amy Foster summarizing these questions and requesting specific labs and a referral check. (If so, confirm whether you want me to send it and provide the PCP’s email or say “yes, draft only.”)
- I can also create a one-page printout you can bring to the visit with the exact questions to ask.

If you confirm what you meant by “72%” (or give your most recent BP reading and recent A1c), I’ll tailor the questions and recommendations further and can draft a concise message to Dr. Foster or a printable checklist for your appointment.

