I can help — this is a good set of things to prepare before your annual physical. First, one clarification: you wrote “My last blood pressure was 72%.” Can you clarify what that means? (Do you mean 72 mmHg for systolic or diastolic, a percentage of a goal, or something else?) That will change some specific recommendations.

Below I’ve organized practical questions to bring to Dr. Amy Foster, recommended tests to request beforehand, what to discuss about medications and interactions, and guidance on when to consider a referral to endocrinology (or cardiology) given your diabetes, heart failure, and age.

Questions to ask your PCP about hypertension management
- What blood pressure target should we aim for given that I have type 2 diabetes and a history of heart failure? (Guidelines commonly use <130/80 mmHg for people with diabetes and CVD but targets should be individualized.)
- Is my current BP regimen (lisinopril 20 mg + metoprolol 25 mg) the best choice for both diabetes and heart failure? Should doses be changed or should another agent be added (thiazide-type diuretic, mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist, or other)? If added, which and why?
- Given my medications and heart-failure history, do you recommend any changes to reduce the risk of low BP/orthostatic hypotension or falls? Should I check orthostatic vitals in clinic now?
- How often should we check kidney function and potassium with my lisinopril? What thresholds would prompt dose changes or stopping the ACE inhibitor?
- Do any of my medications interact in ways that affect BP or increase risk (for example with apixaban or statin)?
- How should I measure BP at home (position, arm, frequency) and what home values should prompt contacting you or going to urgent care?
- If my BP is not at goal, at what point would you refer me to cardiology or a hypertension specialist?
- Would ambulatory (24-hour) BP monitoring be helpful to check for masked or nocturnal hypertension?

Tests and data to have available at the visit
- Recent home BP log (several readings across different days and times).
- Last A1c and recent home glucose readings (fasting/postprandial) or summary from your glucose meter/CGM.
- Labs: A1c, basic metabolic panel (creatinine, eGFR, potassium), urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio, lipid panel, liver tests (ALT/AST), TSH (if not recent).
- Heart-failure monitoring: BNP or NT-proBNP if you’ve had symptoms or change in status; consider ECG and, if indicated, an echocardiogram (ask if it’s time for one).
- Any recent hospitalizations or new symptoms: shortness of breath, increased swelling/edema, lightheadedness, falls.

Medication-specific points to discuss
- Lisinopril: verify kidney function and potassium monitoring schedule and whether this remains the preferred ACEi dose.
- Metoprolol: confirm target dose for heart-failure management vs. BP control and whether dose adjustments are needed.
- Apixaban (5 mg twice daily): confirm dosing is still appropriate for renal function and body weight; ask about bleeding precautions, especially if BP is labile.
- Atorvastatin 40 mg: check most recent LDL and whether high-intensity statin (atorvastatin 40–80) should be continued for secondary prevention.
- Diabetes meds: (I don’t see your diabetes medications listed.) Tell the PCP what you take — if you are not on an SGLT2 inhibitor, ask whether one is appropriate. SGLT2 inhibitors have both diabetes and heart-failure benefits and are often considered when HF + diabetes co-exist.
- Discuss orthostatic symptoms — if you have dizziness, lightheadedness, or syncope, medication timing or doses may need change.

When to consider an endocrinology referral (and what to ask)
- Consider referral if:
  - Your A1c is above your individualized target despite good adherence and lifestyle changes.
  - You have frequent or severe hypoglycemia.
  - You need to start or intensify insulin and would like specialist support.
  - You have complex medication decisions (e.g., simultaneous need for SGLT2 or GLP-1 agonists with significant comorbidities) and want a specialist’s input.
  - You have progressive diabetic complications (CKD, retinopathy, neuropathy) needing coordination.
- What to ask your PCP about a referral:
  - “Given my age (52), type 2 diabetes, and heart-failure history, do you recommend an endocrinology referral now or can you manage my meds and send me to endocrinology only if targets are not met?”
  - “Would you consider initiating/adjusting an SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP-1 agent given my heart-failure history, or would you prefer endocrinology to manage that?”
- Age itself does not automatically require endocrinology — it’s more about control, complications, and complexity.

When to involve cardiology
- If heart-failure symptoms have changed (worse dyspnea, more edema, exercise intolerance), or if BP is hard to control because of heart-failure physiology, ask for cardiology input.
- Ask PCP whether BNP, echo, or cardiology follow-up is recommended as part of your annual review.

Suggested lab/test order to request before or at the visit
- A1c
- CMP (creatinine, eGFR, potassium)
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio
- Lipid panel
- BNP or NT-proBNP (if evaluating HF status)
- ECG; echocardiogram if it’s been >1–2 years or if symptoms changed
- TSH and LFTs if clinically indicated

Sample, short script you can use with Dr. Amy Foster
- “Hi Dr. Foster — for my annual physical I want to review BP goals and medication safety. My conditions: type 2 diabetes, sleep apnea, heart failure. Current meds: apixaban 5 mg BID, metoprolol 25 mg, lisinopril 20 mg, atorvastatin 40 mg. My last BP reading note: [please clarify]. Can we confirm my individualized BP target, review whether my current regimen is optimal (and check kidney function and potassium), and discuss whether I should start or be referred for SGLT2 therapy? Also, do you recommend an endocrinology referral now or only if my A1c is uncontrolled? I can bring my home BP and glucose logs to the visit.”

Safety notes and things to prepare
- If you have any lightheadedness, falls, or pre-syncopal episodes bring that up — it changes BP targets and med timing.
- Bring a complete, up-to-date medication list (including over-the-counter meds and supplements) and your home logs.
- If you want, I can draft a short message/email to Dr. Foster or create a checklist you can print and bring.

If you’d like, tell me:
- What your actual home BP numbers have been (systolic/diastolic), and what you meant by “72%”;
- Your current diabetes medications and last A1c; and
- Any new symptoms (dizziness, swelling, breathlessness).

