This is a good plan — I can help you make a focused list of questions and items to bring up at your annual. A few quick clarifying questions before I make the checklist and suggested wording for your visit or a referral request:

1. You wrote “My last blood pressure was 72%.” Could you clarify what that means — did you mean a reading like 172/72 or 120/72, or that your blood pressure control is 72% of the time, or something else?
2. Do you currently check blood pressure at home? If so, do you have a home BP log or any recent readings you want me to incorporate?
3. Do you want me to draft a short message or referral request to Dr. Amy Foster or to Phoenix Endocrinology that you can copy/paste or send?

Below is a focused checklist and suggested questions to take to your PCP (Dr. Amy Foster) about hypertension management and whether a referral to endocrinology is indicated, based on your history (age 52, type 2 diabetes, sleep apnea, heart failure; current meds: apixaban 5 mg BID, metoprolol 25 mg, lisinopril 20 mg, atorvastatin 40 mg).

What to ask about blood pressure goals and monitoring
- What target blood pressure should we aim for, given my diabetes and heart failure? (Common targets are <130/80 mmHg for many people with diabetes, but this should be individualized.)
- Is my current regimen (lisinopril 20 mg + metoprolol 25 mg) adequate, or should we change/augment it? If so, what would you add and why?
- Could my heart-failure history or my current meds make a lower BP risky (symptoms like dizziness, lightheadedness, or falls)? Should we check orthostatic BPs today (lying, sitting, standing)?
- How often should I measure BP at home and what is the best technique (time of day, position, cuff size)?
- If home readings are above target, what thresholds should prompt me to call (for example, persistent systolic ≥140 mmHg or symptomatic drops)?
- Could my sleep apnea be affecting my blood pressure control? Should we review CPAP adherence or re-titrate therapy?

Medication safety and monitoring to review with your PCP
- Check renal function and electrolytes (BMP: creatinine, eGFR, potassium) because ACE inhibitors (lisinopril) can raise potassium and affect kidney function.
- Check resting HR — beta-blocker dose may need adjustment if bradycardic or symptomatic.
- Review apixaban (bleeding risk) when considering any invasive procedures or if we contemplate adding agents that increase bleeding risk.
- Discuss any possible interactions or side effects between your diabetes meds (if any changes are considered), lisinopril, metoprolol and atorvastatin.
- Ask whether the doses you’re on (metoprolol 25 mg, lisinopril 20 mg) are at target doses for heart-failure benefit or whether cardiology should review doses.

Tests and measurements to request at the visit
- Blood pressure (sitting and standing)
- A1c (diabetes control)
- BMP (creatinine, eGFR, potassium)
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (kidney damage screening)
- Lipid panel (you’re on atorvastatin 40 mg — check LDL and LFTs)
- Weight/BMI and orthostatic vitals if you have symptoms
- ECG or echocardiogram if your heart-failure status hasn’t been recently reviewed (ask Dr. Foster whether cardiology follow-up is needed)

When to consider an endocrinology referral (Phoenix Endocrinology)
Consider referral if any of the following apply:
- A1c is above your agreed target despite medication changes and lifestyle measures (for example, persistent A1c >8% or >8.5% depending on individualized target).
- You have frequent hypoglycemia, complex/unstable glucose patterns, or you need initiation/titration of complex insulin regimens.
- You want specialized input on newer diabetes medications with cardiovascular/renal benefit (SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP‑1 receptor agonists) and how they fit with your heart-failure history and kidney function.
- You need help weighing the risks/benefits of weight-loss medications (GLP‑1s) or have complex comorbidities (heart failure, CKD) that require close coordination.
- Unclear diagnosis (possible secondary causes of hyperglycemia) or you want a second opinion on an intensive diabetes plan.

How to phrase a question about referral to Dr. Foster
- “Given my diabetes, heart failure, and age, should I be referred to endocrinology to review my diabetes medications and consider options like an SGLT2 inhibitor or a GLP‑1 agent? I’d like to understand whether those medicines are appropriate and safe for me.”
- “If you recommend keeping my diabetes care with primary care, can we outline a treatment plan and monitoring schedule for trying any new agents (and who will manage follow-up)?”

Specific medication/therapy points to ask about
- SGLT2 inhibitors (empagliflozin/dapagliflozin): ask whether you’re a candidate — these have benefits for heart failure and kidney outcomes but require assessment of kidney function and infection risks.
- GLP‑1 receptor agonists: ask if helpful for glucose and weight control and whether appropriate with your CV history.
- Whether metoprolol dose is optimal for heart-failure management vs. BP control.
- Whether lisinopril dose is optimal or if switching/adding an agent (e.g., thiazide-like diuretic, calcium-channel blocker) would help — discuss renal monitoring.
- Any drug interactions or safety concerns with current anticoagulation (apixaban) if additional therapies or procedures are planned.

Other preventative care and referrals to consider at the annual visit
- Diabetic eye exam referral (annual retinal exam)
- Foot exam and foot-care advice
- Vaccinations (flu, shingles if eligible, COVID boosters if due, pneumococcal as indicated)
- Sleep apnea review (CPAP adherence) — poor control can worsen BP and glucose control
- Cardiology follow-up if your heart-failure symptoms or functional status changed

Home monitoring plan you can propose
- Measure BP at home twice daily (morning/ evening) for 7 days before the visit; bring the log or upload readings.
- Track fasting and post-prandial glucose or a 2–4 week glucose log if you monitor — bring A1c if you have a recent result.

Would you like:
- I can draft a short message you could send to Dr. Amy Foster summarizing these concerns and asking specifically about a referral to Phoenix Endocrinology.
- Or I can make a one-page checklist/script you can bring to the appointment.

Tell me what you’d like next (and please clarify the “72%” BP note and whether you want me to draft the message).

