Good plan — I can help you get organized for the visit. Below I’ve split this into (A) quick clarifying questions for you, (B) suggested things to bring and tests to request before/at the visit, (C) focused questions to ask Dr. Foster about hypertension management, and (D) focused questions about whether you need a referral to endocrinology (Phoenix Endocrinology). I’ll finish with a short “ask the doctor” script you can read or send.

A — quick clarifying questions
- When you say “My last blood pressure was 72%,” do you mean:
  - a systolic/diastolic reading (e.g., 132/72 mmHg) and one number got truncated, or
  - your blood pressure control was 72% of the time/on-target, or
  - something else?
- Do you have recent lab results (A1c, BMP/creatinine, urine albumin) from the last 3–6 months?
- Are you using CPAP for sleep apnea, and do you have compliance data (hours/night)?

B — what to bring / request before the visit
- A printed/phone list of current meds (apixaban 5 mg BID, metoprolol 25 mg, lisinopril 20 mg, atorvastatin 40 mg, plus OTCs/supplements).
- Home BP log or 1–2 weeks of home readings (morning and evening; note position — sitting/standing).
- Recent labs if available; if not, ask that these be ordered:
  - A1c (glycemic control)
  - Basic metabolic panel (creatinine, eGFR, potassium) — important for apixaban dosing and ACE inhibitor safety
  - Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) — diabetic kidney disease screen
  - Lipid panel (if not recent)
  - BNP or NT-proBNP (helpful for heart failure status)
  - Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) if weight/energy changes
- If you haven’t had one recently: ECG and echocardiogram (for heart failure follow-up) — ask if due.
- If your PCP can’t decide on BP control from office readings, consider asking for ambulatory BP monitoring (ABPM) or to base decisions on home BP averages.

C — questions to ask about hypertension management
- Is my current blood-pressure target the same as usual given my diabetes and heart-failure history? (Many clinicians target <130/80 mmHg in patients with diabetes/ASCVD, but targets are individualized.)
- Are my home BP readings consistent with what you want? (If not, should I do ABPM or continue home logs and when?)
- Are my current meds (lisinopril 20 mg + metoprolol 25 mg) adequate for blood-pressure control, or would you:
  - increase the lisinopril dose,
  - add a thiazide/thiazide-like diuretic (chlorthalidone or HCTZ),
  - add a calcium-channel blocker (amlodipine),
  - or prefer a different adjustment because of my heart failure?
- Given my heart-failure history, are there any specific BP medications you’d avoid or prefer?
- How does my sleep apnea/CPAP adherence factor into BP management? (e.g., should we re-check CPAP usage or refer to sleep medicine if not on CPAP or not adherent)
- Any concerns about interactions or side effects with my anticoagulant (apixaban) or other meds if we change BP meds? (e.g., monitoring potassium/creatinine if we change ACE inhibitor dose)
- How often should I monitor BP at home and when should I call you about readings that worry you? (Ask for exact thresholds)
- Would you like me to keep a home BP log and follow up in X weeks if we make med changes?

D — questions about referral to endocrinology (Phoenix Endocrinology)
Ask Dr. Foster if a referral is recommended if any of the following apply to you — and if the answer is yes, request the referral:
- My A1c is above target despite current therapy (e.g., >7% or your individualized target) or I have frequent hypoglycemia — refer for specialized diabetes management.
- I need complex medication changes (starting insulin, complex insulin titration, or advanced GLP-1/SGLT2 counseling) and PCP prefers specialist co-management.
- I have progressive diabetic complications (worsening albuminuria/CKD, retinopathy, neuropathy) needing specialist input.
- I want a second opinion about adding SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP-1 receptor agonists given my heart-failure history (these classes have heart and kidney benefits and sometimes prompt referral if PCP prefers specialist prescribing).
- I have difficulty with weight management and want access to options like GLP-1 agonists that endocrinologists often manage.
- I have endocrine concerns beyond routine T2D care (e.g., suspicion of secondary causes of hypertension, atypical diabetes features).

Practical phrasing to ask about referral:
- “Given my age (52), type 2 diabetes, sleep apnea, and heart-failure history, would you recommend a referral to an endocrinologist (Phoenix Endocrinology) now — and if not, what specific results or circumstances would prompt you to refer?”

E — safety/red flags to raise at the visit or call immediately
- New/worsening shortness of breath, sudden weight gain (fluid), fainting or near-syncope, chest pain, severe dizziness — seek urgent care.
- If creatinine or potassium rises significantly after medication change, contact office.

F — suggested short script you can use during the visit or in a message
(You can paste this to Dr. Foster’s portal)
“Hi Dr. Foster — ahead of my annual physical I want to review blood-pressure management and whether I should see endocrinology. I have T2D, sleep apnea, and prior heart failure; my meds are apixaban 5 mg BID, metoprolol 25 mg, lisinopril 20 mg, atorvastatin 40 mg. Please order A1c, CMP, UACR, lipid panel, and BNP if appropriate. My home BP readings are [paste values] — is my target still <130/80 for me, and should we change medications now or get ABPM? Given my diabetes and heart failure, would you recommend a referral to Phoenix Endocrinology (or would you like to manage first)? Thanks.”

If you want, I can:
- turn the above into a concise message to send to Dr. Amy Foster through your portal, or
- draft a checklist you can bring to the visit, or
- help interpret recent lab values or home BP logs if you paste them here.

