Good plan — I can help you prepare a focused agenda for your annual physical with Dr. Amy Foster. Below I’ve organized suggested questions and items to bring/ask by topic (hypertension, diabetes/endocrinology referral, meds, heart failure, tests & monitoring, and next steps). I also flagged one item I need you to confirm.

Quick clarification
- You wrote “My last blood pressure was 72%.” Can you confirm what that means (for example: a reading like 132/72, or a pulse of 72, or something else)? I’ll tailor the questions once I know the actual BP reading.

What to bring to the appointment
- A 1–2 week home BP log (measure morning and evening, seated, 1–2 readings each time)
- Current medication list (names, doses, timing) and any over-the-counter meds/supplements
- Recent lab results (if you have them), A1c, BMP/creatinine, LDL, urine albumin
- Notes about symptoms (dizziness, lightheadedness on standing, palpitations, shortness of breath, swelling)
- Any recent hospitalizations, cardiology notes, or imaging (echo report if available)

Questions and topics to cover — Hypertension management
- What is my current BP goal given my type 2 diabetes and history of heart failure? (Ask whether target should be <130/80 vs another goal based on tolerance)
- Is my current regimen (lisinopril 20 mg + metoprolol 25 mg) still the best choice? If not, what changes would you recommend and why?
- Given my sleep apnea and diabetes, could my BP be improved by addressing sleep apnea (CPAP adherence/adjustment)?
- Should we check orthostatic vitals today (supine → standing) to screen for orthostatic hypotension before adjusting meds?
- Would shifting any medication timing (morning vs evening dosing) help nighttime BP control or side effects?
- If my home readings remain above goal, what is the next medication you would add? (Ask about thiazide-like diuretics, calcium-channel blockers, MRAs — and the rationale)
- Are there drug interactions or bleeding-risk concerns with my anticoagulant (apixaban) if we intensify BP therapy?
- How often should I monitor home BP and when should I call you about high/low readings?

Questions and topics — Diabetes & need for endocrinology referral
- Based on my age (52), T2D, and heart-failure history, would you recommend referral to Phoenix Endocrinology now or only if my control changes? (Ask for their referral thresholds.)
- Specific reasons to consider a referral: difficulty reaching A1c target, recurrent hypoglycemia, need for complex insulin initiation/titration, consideration of GLP-1 receptor agonist or SGLT2 inhibitor initiation, advanced complications (nephropathy, retinopathy, neuropathy), or need for advanced diabetes tech (CGM/insulin pump).
- Given my heart failure and diabetes, should I be on an SGLT2 inhibitor (eg empagliflozin/dapagliflozin) for both glucose and heart-failure benefit? If not, why? If recommended, can you start it or refer to endocrinology/cardiology to initiate safely?
- Would a GLP-1 receptor agonist be appropriate for cardiovascular risk reduction or weight management in my case?
- Ask: What A1c target should I aim for? (Discuss individualized target, balancing hypoglycemia risk with benefit.)
- Are there diabetes complications I should have screened urgently (urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio, retinal exam, neuropathy/foot exam)?

Medication review & safety checks
- Please review all meds for interactions (particularly apixaban + any new agents) and dosing based on current renal function and weight.
- Is apixaban 5 mg BID still appropriate based on my kidney function/age/weight?
- Any lab monitoring needed if we change or add meds (electrolytes, creatinine, liver enzymes, A1c, lipid panel)?
- Discuss beta-blocker (metoprolol) dose in context of heart-failure management and symptomatic bradycardia/fatigue.

Heart failure-specific items
- What is my most recent left ventricular ejection fraction (LVEF)? Do you recommend a cardiology follow-up or updated echo?
- Am I on optimal guideline-directed medical therapy for heart failure (ACEi/ARB/ARNI, beta-blocker, MRA, SGLT2 as appropriate)? If not, what changes are indicated?
- Should we check BNP/NT-proBNP or order an updated echo now?
- Any diuretic adjustments needed for fluid status or edema?

Recommended tests to order (if not done recently)
- A1c
- Basic metabolic panel (electrolytes, creatinine, eGFR)
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio
- Fasting lipid panel
- Liver function tests (if considering GLP-1 or other agents)
- ECG (if no recent one) and echocardiogram (if >1 year or symptom change)
- BNP/NT-proBNP (if heart-failure management questions)
- Retinal screening referral if not up to date
- Consider TSH if clinically indicated

Lifestyle and sleep apnea
- Discuss CPAP adherence and whether optimizing sleep apnea treatment could improve BP and glucose control
- Review sodium intake, weight management, exercise tolerance, and alcohol use — and how they link to BP and HF risk

How to decide about endocrinology referral (practical criteria to ask your PCP)
- Consider referral if any of the following apply:
  - A1c persistently above goal despite primary-care management or multiple medication adjustments
  - Recurrent hypoglycemia or complex insulin needs
  - Need for initiation of advanced glucose-lowering therapies (GLP-1 RA, SGLT2) with comorbidities that need close coordination
  - Rapid progression of microvascular complications (nephropathy, retinopathy)
  - Patient preference for specialist management or limited time in PCP visits for diabetes complexity

Suggested phrasing for your visit
- “Given my diabetes, sleep apnea, and heart-failure history, what BP goal should I be aiming for, and is my current regimen the best way to meet that goal?”
- “Can we review my kidney function and labs today to make sure apixaban and any new diabetes drugs are dosed safely?”
- “Would you recommend a referral to Phoenix Endocrinology now? If not, what specific thresholds would trigger a referral?”
- “Should I start home BP monitoring and bring a 2-week log? What values should prompt me to call you?”

Next steps I can help with
- Draft a concise email or message to Dr. Amy Foster listing these questions and requesting labs/echo if you want (I can draft it and leave it as a draft or send it if you ask).
- Create a printable one-page visit agenda and two-week BP log template for you to use.
- If you confirm the actual blood pressure reading (or clarify what “72%” means), I’ll refine the goals and suggested talking points further.

Would you like me to draft a short message to Dr. Foster summarizing these concerns and requesting any specific tests or a referral to Phoenix Endocrinology? And please confirm your last BP reading so I can tailor the recommendations.

