Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can help you get ready. A couple quick clarifying questions before the detailed checklist:
- When you say “My last blood pressure was 72%,” what do you mean? (Did you mean 72 mmHg for systolic or diastolic, 72% medication adherence, or something else?)
- What are your current diabetes medicines and most recent A1c (if you know them)? That will affect whether an endocrinology referral is likely.

Below I’ve organized practical items to take to your annual with Dr. Amy Foster, focused on hypertension management and whether to request a referral to Phoenix Endocrinology.

1) High-priority checks and tests to have done before/at the visit
- A1c (if not done in last 3 months)
- Basic metabolic panel (creatinine, eGFR, potassium) — especially important because you’re on lisinopril
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (diabetes + ACE inhibitor monitoring)
- Lipid panel (you’re on atorvastatin but check LDL)
- Office and/or ambulatory/home BP log (see home BP notes below)
- Weight, orthostatic vitals (sit and stand BP/HR) if you have dizziness/lightheadedness
- BNP or NT-proBNP if heart-failure status is uncertain or to guide management (ask PCP/cardiology)
- Foot exam and referral for retinal screening if not current
- Medication reconciliation (bring a list of all meds, doses — including OTCs, supplements)

2) Home BP monitoring — bring readings to the visit
- Take BP twice daily (morning and evening) for 7 days; take 2 measurements 1 minute apart and record average
- Sit quietly for 5 minutes before measurement; use a validated upper-arm cuff
- Bring or paste the log into the visit message — trends matter more than a single reading

3) Questions to ask Dr. Foster about hypertension management
- What BP target do you recommend for me given my diabetes, history of heart failure, and age? (e.g., is <130/80 appropriate/tolerable?)
- My current meds are lisinopril 20 mg and metoprolol 25 mg — are these the best choices, or should we consider adding/changing agents? (examples to discuss: thiazide-type diuretic, calcium-channel blocker, mineralocorticoid antagonist)
