Thanks — this is a good start. A couple quick clarifying items before I make specific suggestions:
- When you wrote “My last blood pressure was 72%,” do you mean your last BP reading (e.g., 172/92) or that your blood-pressure control was 72% of the time? If you can give the actual most recent systolic/diastolic reading (or a typical home-BP range), I can tailor the targets/concerns better.
- Do you know your most recent A1c, eGFR (or creatinine), and whether you’re taking any diabetes meds besides what you listed (you didn’t list a diabetes drug)? Also, are you using CPAP for sleep apnea and is it effective?

Below are targeted questions, testing/lab items, and decision points you can use at your annual with Dr. Amy Foster to address hypertension management and whether you need a referral to Phoenix Endocrinology.

High-level framing to say at the visit
- “I’d like to review my blood pressure and diabetes care before we finalize my plan today. I want to understand my BP target given my age, diabetes, sleep apnea, and heart-failure history, and whether I should see an endocrinologist.”

Questions about hypertension management (ask Dr. Foster)
- What blood-pressure target do you recommend for me given my age (52), type 2 diabetes, sleep apnea, and heart-failure history (what target systolic/diastolic and any caveats)?
- Are my current BP medications appropriate? (I’m taking lisinopril 20 mg and metoprolol 25 mg.) Should any doses be adjusted or should we consider adding a calcium-channel blocker, thiazide-type diuretic, or other agent?
- Could my sleep apnea be affecting my BP control? Should we review CPAP adherence or refer to sleep-medicine for optimization?
- Do you think I need ambulatory (24-hour) BP monitoring or a structured home BP log to confirm control and exclude white-coat or masked hypertension?
- Should we check orthostatic vitals to assess for medication-related low BP or dizziness risk?
- Are there interactions or safety concerns with my anticoagulant (apixaban) and my BP regimen I should watch for (e.g., falls, bleeding risk with very low BP)?
- How often should I monitor home BP and report readings to you? What thresholds should prompt me to call or to go to urgent care?
- If my BP isn’t at goal, at what point do you prefer medication changes vs. lifestyle measures first?

Medications and safety checks
- Review all meds and OTCs/supplements for interactions and additive effects on BP or heart rate.
- Check renal function and potassium because I’m on an ACE inhibitor (lisinopril): “Can we check BMP (creatinine, eGFR, potassium) since I’m on lisinopril?”
- If on metoprolol, ask about heart-rate targets and whether beta-blocker dose is appropriate in heart-failure context.

Labs, tests, and data to request
- Recent A1c (and target A1c) and fasting glucose or glucose log
- Basic metabolic panel (BMP) including creatinine/eGFR and potassium
- Lipid panel (you’re on atorvastatin 40 mg)
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (kidney microalbumin)
- ECG if not recently done (given anticoagulant/heart history)
- If heart-failure symptoms or unclear control: BNP or NT-proBNP, and consider cardiology follow-up or echo if not recent
- Ambulatory 24-hour BP or home BP log if readings vary or there’s concern for uncontrolled BP

When to consider referral to an endocrinologist (Phoenix Endocrinology)
Consider asking for a referral if any of the following apply — these are reasonable triggers to request specialty input:
- A1c above your target despite treatment adjustments (especially if A1c remains high or you’re on multiple agents).
- Frequent or severe hypoglycemia or unstable glucose variability.
- Complexity of diabetes regimen (e.g., starting or adjusting insulin, GLP-1 agonist, SGLT2 inhibitor) where specialty input is desired.
- Difficulty achieving glycemic targets with primary-care management or multiple comorbidities (heart failure, CKD).
- New or progressive diabetes complications (neuropathy, retinopathy, kidney disease, or hospitalization for hyperglycemia).
- You want a second opinion on advanced therapies (insulin pump, continuous glucose monitoring adjustments, or newer drugs with cardio-renal benefits).

Questions to ask the PCP about referral
- “Given my age, diabetes, heart-failure history, and current control, would you recommend a referral to Phoenix Endocrinology? What specific goals or problems would justify that referral?”
- “If we refer, what information should I bring (recent labs, medication list, home glucose/BP logs)?”
- “Would endocrinology likely change my meds (e.g., start SGLT2 or GLP-1) because of my heart-failure risk and diabetes?”

Heart-failure–specific considerations to raise
- Confirm whether heart-failure management is optimized (meds, recent ejection fraction if known).
- Ask how blood-pressure targets or med choices differ because of heart failure.
- Discuss whether any diabetes drugs with cardiovascular or renal benefit (e.g., SGLT2 inhibitors) are appropriate given heart-failure history and kidney function — this is a topic PCP and endocrinology may collaborate on.

Sleep apnea and BP
- Tell the PCP your CPAP usage and whether symptoms persist (daytime sleepiness). Ask if improving CPAP adherence or re-titration could improve BP.
- Ask whether sleep-medicine re-evaluation would help BP control.

Pre-visit checklist to bring to Dr. Foster
- Complete, current medication list (name, dose, frequency) including supplements and OTC meds.
- Home BP log for the past 2–4 weeks (morning and evening readings) or summary averages.
- Home glucose readings or CGM printout if used; most recent A1c.
- Any recent labs or notes from other specialists (cardiology, sleep clinic).
- List of symptoms (dizziness, lightheadedness, falls, chest pain, edema, shortness of breath, hypoglycemia episodes).
- Questions you want answered (print the list above or I can format them into a one-page sheet).

Sample, short way to ask Dr. Foster in-clinic or by message
- “Hi Dr. Foster — for my annual, I’d like to review BP and diabetes management. My main questions: what BP target should I have given my diabetes and heart-failure history, are my current BP meds optimal, and would you recommend a referral to Phoenix Endocrinology? I can bring my home BP log and recent labs.”

Follow-up options I can help with
- I can format the above into a one-page printout or into an email/draft message to Dr. Foster requesting the items and referral. Would you like me to draft that message?
- If you can share your most recent A1c and a typical home-BP reading (or clarify what “72%” meant), I can give more tailored questions and suggested target ranges to discuss.

